<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================================
  OutlookAI testbed - unattended Windows 11 install answer file TEMPLATE.

  THIS IS A TEMPLATE. IT CONTAINS NO CREDENTIAL AND MUST NEVER CONTAIN ONE.
  This repository is public and a guest password has already been published from it once; it
  survives in git history and had to be rotated. Every credential appears here only as a
  placeholder token written in double braces. Testbed/host/New-AnswerFile.ps1 substitutes the
  real values, reading them from the gitignored vm-credentials.json that
  Testbed/host/Get-GuestCredential.ps1 documents, and writes the filled autounattend.xml into
  gitignored scratch (.work/) only. Nothing filled ever comes back into Testbed/.
  .github/scripts/check-testbed-references.ps1 check 7 fails the build if the placeholders in
  this file are ever replaced by anything that looks like a value.

  Placeholders, all substituted by New-AnswerFile.ps1. They are named here without their braces
  on purpose, so that this comment block is not itself rewritten when the file is filled in:
      GUEST_USERNAME   the local administrator account the testbed runs as
      GUEST_PASSWORD   that account's password
      COMPUTER_NAME    the guest's machine name
      IMAGE_NAME       the edition to install out of the multi-edition ISO

  ============================================================================================
  WHAT THIS FILE ENCODES, AND WHY THESE VALUES

  The guest must match the MAINTAINER'S OWN MACHINE, not some notional clean default. That
  configuration is where most of the userbase sits, so it is what the live tier has to be
  testing against. The table below was MEASURED on the host (PC657) on 2026-08-25; see
  Testbed/MEDIA.md, section "The host configuration the guests match", for the full survey and
  the command each value was read with.

      OS                       Windows 11 Pro 10.0.26200 (25H2)
      Base install language    en-US (OSLanguage 1033)
      MUI languages present    en-US, en-GB, nl-NL
      Effective display lang   en-GB          from Get-UICulture
      Preferred language list  en-NL, nl-NL   from Get-WinUserLanguageList
      System locale (ANSI)     en-US          from Get-WinSystemLocale
      User locale / formats    nl-NL          from Get-Culture (d-M-yyyy, 4.000,50, EUR, Monday)
      Home location            Netherlands, GeoId 176
      Keyboard, both languages KLID 00020409, United States-International
      Time zone                W. Europe Standard Time (UTC+01:00 Amsterdam), DST on

  THE en-GB LINE IS THE ONE THAT SURPRISES PEOPLE. The host's first preferred language is
  en-NL, English (Netherlands). Windows ships no MUI for en-NL, so the display language falls
  back to en-GB, which is why Get-UICulture says en-GB on a machine whose language list never
  mentions it. That makes the staged English International image (volume label
  CCCOMA_X64FRE_EN-GB_DV9) the CORRECT base image for matching this host, not a mismatch to be
  corrected.

  A CONSEQUENCE THE TEST CORPUS MUST SURVIVE, STATED ON PURPOSE: with UserLocale nl-NL the
  guest renders four thousand and a half as 4.000,50 and today as 25-8-2026. That is
  deliberate. The remediation console once printed 4.000 for four thousand on a Dutch-locale
  machine and had to be pinned to the invariant culture; the guests reproduce exactly the
  conditions that found it.

  ============================================================================================
  WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT EXPRESS, AND WHERE THAT IS FIXED

  en-NL is a TRANSIENT language. Windows assigns it a transient LCID out of the 0x2000 block at
  runtime, so no answer file can name it and no answer file can put it at the head of the
  language list. The InputLocale values below therefore use en-GB (0809), the MUI that en-NL
  actually resolves to, paired with the United States-International layout; the final list is
  set at first logon by Testbed/guest/Complete-FirstLogon.ps1, which calls
  Set-WinUserLanguageList with en-NL then nl-NL and forces 00020409 on both. That script also
  sets the home location (GeoId 176), re-asserts the system locale and the user culture,
  disables fast startup and stops the machine ever sleeping. A testbed that sleeps mid-run is a
  mystery failure, not a test result.

  ============================================================================================
  NO BYPASS HACKS. There is deliberately no LabConfig, BypassTPMCheck, BypassSecureBootCheck or
  BypassRAMCheck registry work anywhere in this file. A Generation 2 Hyper-V VM with a virtual
  TPM and Secure Boot enabled satisfies Windows 11's requirements natively, so there is nothing
  to bypass. Testbed/host/New-TestbedVm.ps1 enables both (Set-VMKeyProtector, Enable-VMTPM,
  Set-VMFirmware -EnableSecureBoot On). A guest built by switching the checks off is a
  different machine from the one the userbase runs, which defeats the point of matching the
  host.

  LOCAL ACCOUNT, NOT A MICROSOFT ACCOUNT. Two mechanisms, and it is worth knowing which one
  does the work. The one relied on is the SUPPORTED one: UserAccounts/LocalAccounts in
  oobeSystem creates a local administrator outright, and OOBE/HideOnlineAccountScreens stops
  OOBE asking for a Microsoft account at all. The BypassNRO registry value set from
  Microsoft-Windows-Deployment in the specialize pass is belt and braces for the "let's connect
  you to a network" page on a guest whose network adapter is deliberately disconnected. Note
  that Windows 11 25H2 no longer ships oobe\bypassnro.cmd, so the Shift+F10 recipe found in
  older notes does not exist on this image. Nothing here depends on it.

  DISK LAYOUT is GPT for Generation 2: EFI system partition, Microsoft Reserved, then Windows
  filling the rest. Disk 0 is wiped. The answer-file volume is attached as a second DVD drive
  and NOT as a second disk, precisely so that "disk 0" stays unambiguous; see
  Testbed/host/New-AnswerFile.ps1 for the rest of that reasoning.
  ============================================================================================
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend"
          xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
          xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">

  <settings pass="windowsPE">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <!-- Setup's own UI. en-GB because that is the MUI on this image and the one the host
           effectively runs. -->
      <SetupUILanguage>
        <UILanguage>en-GB</UILanguage>
      </SetupUILanguage>
      <!-- language:keyboard-layout pairs. 0809 is en-GB, 0413 is nl-NL, both on KLID
           00020409, United States-International, which is what the host has for both of its
           languages. en-NL cannot appear here: its LCID is transient.
           Complete-FirstLogon.ps1 replaces this list with en-NL then nl-NL at first logon. -->
      <InputLocale>0809:00020409;0413:00020409</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>en-GB</UILanguage>
      <UILanguageFallback>en-US</UILanguageFallback>
      <UserLocale>nl-NL</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">

      <!-- GPT layout for a Generation 2 (UEFI) VM. Sizes are in MB. A 260 MB EFI system
           partition is Microsoft's own recommendation for advanced-format disks. -->
      <DiskConfiguration>
        <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        <Disk wcm:action="add">
          <DiskID>0</DiskID>
          <WillWipeDisk>true</WillWipeDisk>
          <CreatePartitions>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order>
              <Type>EFI</Type>
              <Size>260</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order>
              <Type>MSR</Type>
              <Size>16</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order>
              <Type>Primary</Type>
              <Extend>true</Extend>
            </CreatePartition>
          </CreatePartitions>
          <ModifyPartitions>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order>
              <PartitionID>1</PartitionID>
              <Label>System</Label>
              <Format>FAT32</Format>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <!-- The MSR partition is created and then left entirely alone: never formatted,
                 never lettered. -->
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order>
              <PartitionID>2</PartitionID>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order>
              <PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
              <Label>Windows</Label>
              <Letter>C</Letter>
              <Format>NTFS</Format>
            </ModifyPartition>
          </ModifyPartitions>
        </Disk>
      </DiskConfiguration>

      <ImageInstall>
        <OSImage>
          <!-- The staged ISO is consumer multi-edition. Naming the image is what stops Setup
               showing the edition picker. -->
          <InstallFrom>
            <MetaData wcm:action="add">
              <Key>/IMAGE/NAME</Key>
              <Value>{{IMAGE_NAME}}</Value>
            </MetaData>
          </InstallFrom>
          <InstallTo>
            <DiskID>0</DiskID>
            <PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
          </InstallTo>
          <InstallToAvailablePartition>false</InstallToAvailablePartition>
          <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        </OSImage>
      </ImageInstall>

      <UserData>
        <AcceptEula>true</AcceptEula>
        <!-- EMPTY ON PURPOSE. An empty Key skips the product-key page; the edition comes from
             /IMAGE/NAME above. An unactivated consumer Pro install has no expiry at all, which
             is exactly what Testbed/MEDIA.md decided to rely on: it watermarks and nags but it
             never stops, so Windows is never the reason to rebuild. Office's 30-day grace is
             the binding clock. -->
        <ProductKey>
          <Key></Key>
          <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        </ProductKey>
        <FullName>OutlookAI Testbed</FullName>
        <Organization>OutlookAI Testbed</Organization>
      </UserData>

      <EnableFirewall>true</EnableFirewall>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="specialize">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <ComputerName>{{COMPUTER_NAME}}</ComputerName>
      <TimeZone>W. Europe Standard Time</TimeZone>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Deployment"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <RunSynchronous>
        <!-- BypassNRO, set in SPECIALIZE rather than in windowsPE deliberately: in windowsPE
             the HKLM hive belongs to WinPE and the value would be thrown away with it, whereas
             in specialize HKLM is the installed system that OOBE will actually read. This is
             belt and braces behind HideOnlineAccountScreens; it is what gives OOBE an "I do
             not have internet" route on a guest whose network adapter is disconnected by
             design. -->
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Description>Allow OOBE to finish without a network or a Microsoft account.</Description>
          <Path>reg add HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\OOBE /v BypassNRO /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
      </RunSynchronous>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <settings pass="oobeSystem">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <InputLocale>0809:00020409;0413:00020409</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>en-GB</UILanguage>
      <UILanguageFallback>en-US</UILanguageFallback>
      <UserLocale>nl-NL</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">

      <TimeZone>W. Europe Standard Time</TimeZone>

      <UserAccounts>
        <!-- A LOCAL administrator. This is the supported route to a local account, and it is
             what the testbed depends on: PowerShell Direct lands in session 0, Outlook can
             never finish starting there, and the only way to reach session 1 is a scheduled
             task registered with LogonType=Interactive landing in a session that already
             exists. See Testbed/guest/Register-InteractiveTask.ps1. No console session, no
             live tier. -->
        <LocalAccounts>
          <LocalAccount wcm:action="add">
            <Name>{{GUEST_USERNAME}}</Name>
            <DisplayName>{{GUEST_USERNAME}}</DisplayName>
            <Group>Administrators</Group>
            <Description>OutlookAI testbed guest account, created by Testbed/host/New-AnswerFile.ps1.</Description>
            <Password>
              <Value>{{GUEST_PASSWORD}}</Value>
              <PlainText>true</PlainText>
            </Password>
          </LocalAccount>
        </LocalAccounts>
      </UserAccounts>

      <!-- Autologon is what keeps session 1 alive for the interactive task to land in.
           LogonCount is 999 rather than 1: Windows decrements it on every logon and switches
           autologon off when it reaches zero, and a guest that silently stops auto-logging-on
           after one reboot presents as a live tier that hangs. State the consequence rather
           than discover it later: while the count is above zero, Windows keeps the password in
           the registry under Winlogon\DefaultPassword in clear text. That is a property of
           autologon, it is already true of the existing guest, and it is one more reason the
           testbed VM is disposable and holds nothing that matters. -->
      <AutoLogon>
        <Enabled>true</Enabled>
        <Username>{{GUEST_USERNAME}}</Username>
        <LogonCount>999</LogonCount>
        <Password>
          <Value>{{GUEST_PASSWORD}}</Value>
          <PlainText>true</PlainText>
        </Password>
      </AutoLogon>

      <OOBE>
        <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
        <HideOEMRegistrationScreen>true</HideOEMRegistrationScreen>
        <HideOnlineAccountScreens>true</HideOnlineAccountScreens>
        <HideLocalAccountScreen>true</HideLocalAccountScreen>
        <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
        <NetworkLocation>Work</NetworkLocation>
        <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>
      </OOBE>

      <FirstLogonCommands>
        <!-- The fix-ups the answer file cannot express live in a real script rather than in
             XML, so they can be read, reviewed and changed in git:
             Testbed/guest/Complete-FirstLogon.ps1, which New-AnswerFile.ps1 puts on the
             answer-file volume beside this file. That volume's drive letter is not knowable in
             advance, so the command looks for the script on every filesystem drive instead of
             assuming one, and writes a log either way. The ampersand is XML-escaped; it is the
             PowerShell call operator. -->
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Description>Set the language list, home location, locales, power policy and fast startup.</Description>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
          <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "$f = Get-PSDrive -PSProvider FileSystem | ForEach-Object { Join-Path $_.Root 'Complete-FirstLogon.ps1' } | Where-Object { Test-Path -LiteralPath $_ } | Select-Object -First 1; if ($f) { &amp; $f } else { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path 'C:\Windows\Setup' | Out-Null; 'Complete-FirstLogon.ps1 was not found on any drive. The guest locale and power settings are NOT the ones the testbed expects.' | Set-Content -LiteralPath 'C:\Windows\Setup\first-logon.log' }"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
      </FirstLogonCommands>
    </component>
  </settings>
</unattend>
